#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# check_arguments

#% text
# `check_arguments(f(a1, ...), n)` is `true` when the call has the right number of arguments, the options after them not counted, and `false` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` is the number of arguments, or `[min, max]` (`max` may be `inf`).

#% item
# Otherwise the result is `false`: it serves as a condition (`rule f(args___) := ... when check_arguments(f(args), 2)`). `arguments_options` gives the reason, `failure(argument_count, f, given, n)`.

#% item
# The call is held, as for `arguments_options`, and the options after the arguments are not counted; the settings of `arguments_options` (`options_mode`, an association third argument) apply.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
check_arguments(f(1, a -> 0), 1)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
check_arguments(f(1, 2, 3), [2, inf])
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Options do not count unless `options_mode: none`, and a call that is not a call of a head fails:

#% cell
[check_arguments(f(1, a -> 2, b -> 3), 1), check_arguments(f(1, a -> 2), 2, options_mode: none), check_arguments(5, 1)]
#% output
#= [true, true, false]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# It is the test of the failure of `arguments_options`:

#% cell
[check_arguments(f(1, 2, 3), [1, 2]), is_failure(arguments_options(f(1, 2, 3), [1, 2]))]
#% output
#= [false, true]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Too few:

#% cell
check_arguments(f(1), [2, inf])
#% output
#= false

#% text
# The reason:

#% cell
arguments_options(f(1), [2, inf])
#% output
#! failure(argument_count, f, 1, [2, inf])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `arguments_options`
